# Line endings a file has to have whatever a clone's `core.autocrlf` says. Git for Windows installs
# with it on, and bash reads a carriage return as part of the line: a bash driver stopped at
# `set -euo pipefail` with "invalid option name", and every value a sourced file set carried one.
# `omw` is started through its first line, where a carriage return would name another program.
# A batch file is cmd's, which reads its lines as CR LF.
omw text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
# The integration suites' data is read back byte for byte: `test_lua_api`'s `vfs` test reads
# `lines.txt` as `1`, `2`, ``, `4`, and on a clone that converts it reads `1` and a carriage return.
scripts/data/integration_tests/** -text
# C++ is LF on every clone, as `.clang-format`'s `LineEnding` writes it, so a Windows clone formats
# byte for byte as a Linux one. A merge lays out the base and both sides with the pinned clang-format
# first (`omw merge-file`, which `upstream.yml` sets as the driver), so an upstream edit to a file the
# fork reformatted conflicts only where the edit does; a clone with no such driver merges as git does.
*.cpp text eol=lf merge=clang-format
*.hpp text eol=lf merge=clang-format
*.h text eol=lf merge=clang-format
# GLSL too: a module compiled with its source (`--shader-source`) carries the file's bytes.
*.glsl text eol=lf
*.vert text eol=lf
*.frag text eol=lf
*.comp text eol=lf
*.rgen text eol=lf
# Vendored code keeps what its project wrote, and so do the vcpkg ports copied from their registries, whose
# patches git applies byte for byte: a Windows clone's carriage returns corrupted them.
extern/** !text !eol !merge
vcpkg/ports/** -text
